Input: 
nums = [1,2,3,4]
Output: 
[24,12,8,6]

Input: 
nums = [-1,1,0,-3,3]
Output: 
[0,0,9,0,0]

Steps:
We are creating 3 arrays to find products from left as l[], right as r[] and the resultant array as prod[].
First we initialise l[0] and r[n-1] as 1 because there is no element in the left and right of the first and last element.
Then we calculate product of nums[i-1] and l[i-1] and store in l[i], since we should find the product of every elements left to ith element except for itself.
Similarly for r[], calculate product of nums[i+1] and r[i+1] to find product of elements from right of the ith element.
Then multiply l[i] and r[i] to find result.

Code:
class Solution {
    public int[] productExceptSelf(int[] nums) {
        int n=nums.length;
        int[] prod=new int[n];
        int[] l=new int[n];
        int[] r=new int[n];
        l[0]=1;
        r[n-1]=1;
        for(int i=1;i<n;i++){
            l[i]=nums[i-1]*l[i-1];
        }
        for(int i=n-2;i>=0;i--){
            r[i]=nums[i+1]*r[i+1];
        }
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            prod[i]=l[i]*r[i];
        }
        return prod;
    }
}
